content.text:
User: "when you scroll to the windows for the first time, animations are there, but when [you do it] the second time they're already loaded without animations." So indeed: it's the Reveal animation of the preview windows (screenshots/panels) when scrolling away and back — animations don't replay. They want them to replay.

"на окна листаешь" = scroll to the windows (the screenshot frames). So fix Reveal to re-trigger each time it enters the viewport.

Implementation: Modify Reveal to keep observing and toggle `is-in` based on intersection, instead of disconnecting after first reveal. Need to make sure repeated scrolling doesn't cause flicker; IntersectionObserver with threshold 0.05 and rootMargin bottom -8%. When leaving the viewport (above or below), remove `is-in`; when entering, add. For tall sections, if they remain partially visible, stays.

Potential flicker on boundary. Use `entry.isIntersecting`. When element scrolled out above → set shown false → transform translateY(20px) i.e., it shifts down while off-screen; scrolling back up it animates from below. That's the desired "replay".

But careful: elements that are taller than the viewport and scroll out only partially. Fine.

Also consider prefers-reduced-motion — CSS handles.

However, there's a subtlety: toggling off while off-screen means the transition plays off-screen, then when re-entering animate. Good.

But if user scrolls up slowly, element at top becomes non-intersecting only when fully out due to rootMargin -8% bottom (root margin only shrinks the bottom by 8%, so the top edge is at viewport top). Fine.

Another subtlety: when the user jumps to a section via nav link, smooth scroll passes over sections quickly, each triggers. Fine.

Also the initial mount: shown false → observer callback async → shown true → transition. Good.

Should I add a small delay before hiding to avoid flicker? Not necessary.

But wait — the user specifically mentions "окна" (windows) — the Reveal variant="window" elements. Should the replay apply to all reveals or only windows? They say "на окна листаешь" — they scroll to the windows. Probably all reveals, consistent. But replaying all text reveals on every scroll might be more distracting. The user wants animation each time they scroll back. Consistent behavior for all Reveal is simpler and expected.

Hmm, but there's a UX concern: elements re-animating every time you scroll past could feel excessive. But user explicitly asks. Implementation could re-arm only after fully leaving the viewport (which is what we do).

Additional concern: The hero uses hero-enter (CSS animation) not Reveal; does the user mean the hero window? "на окна листаешь" — scrolling to the windows. Hero window is visible on load. The Preview windows are `.reveal--window`. So Reveal.

Let me also double check the `Reveal` implementation to toggle properly. Rewrite:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const node = ref.current;
  if (!node) return;
  if (typeof IntersectionObserver === 'undefined') {
    setShown(true);
    return;
  }
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      entries.forEach((entry) => {
        setShown(entry.isIntersecting);
      });
    },
    { rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px', threshold: 0.05 },
  );
  observer.observe(node);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, []);
```

Wait: threshold 0.05 means it needs 5% of the element visible. If an element is taller than the viewport (e.g., the Preview shell? no, it's about 700px, viewport 800), threshold 0.05 of a tall element — if element height is 2000px, 5% = 100px visible needed. Could be fine. But for very tall elements, if 5% never visible? A tall section can have 100px visible. OK.

But for resetting: when isIntersecting false. IntersectionObserver reports entries when crossing thresholds; isIntersecting false when ratio below threshold? Actually with threshold 0.05, the callback fires when intersection ratio crosses 0.05 in either direction, and `isIntersecting` is false when ratio < threshold? Per spec, `isIntersecting` is true if intersecting at all (ratio > 0 or edge-adjacent), independent of thresholds. Actually: "isIntersecting: true if the target intersects the root, even by a pixel." Thresholds only control when callbacks fire. So entering by 1px triggers callback (if ratio crosses 0.05? no—threshold crossing only fires on crossing; initial 0→0.001 doesn't cross 0.05). Hmm: with threshold 0.05, callback fires when ratio goes from <0.05 to >=0.05 and vice versa. When leaving, ratio goes from >=0.05 to <0.05 while still partially visible (isIntersecting true at that moment? at ratio 0.04, still intersecting, isIntersecting true). Then we'd setShown(true) still. When it hits 0, another threshold? Thresholds are [0.05]; crossing 0 → callback? Yes, threshold list implicitly includes 0? The spec: thresholds list is sorted, and 0 is always included? Actually if you specify [0.05], intersection ratio 0 is also observed (the spec says threshold 0 is always added implicitly? Not exactly). To be safe, use `threshold: 0.05` with isIntersecting checks keeps true while any part visible — fine, no flicker. When fully out → isIntersecting false → reset.

But when re-entering, callback fires at ratio 0.05 and isIntersecting true → shown true. Good.

Edge case: on mount, element already visible → initial callback fires with isIntersecting true → shown. Good. Element out of view → callback with false → shown remains false.

Potential issue: when hiding (setShown(false)) while the element is off-screen, the transition re-runs. Then the element is at translateY(20px) offscreen. Re-entry triggers. Good.

But there's a subtle issue with the observer and elements that never fully leave (e.g., huge sections): they'd stay shown. That's fine.

Now, also "при повторном возвращении" might include navigating back to the page? Routes unmount/remount so replays.

Also the user might want *windows* to animate when returning to the tab (browser). Should I add visibilitychange replay? Not asked; skip.

Let me now implement, then verify in the browser by scrolling away and back and checking `is-in` is removed and animation transitions run. Test with the dev server hot reload.

Also possibly the user's complaint is about the Preview tab images? No — they clarified scrolling to windows.

One more consideration: `Reveal` with `variant="window"` wraps the whole Preview section shell. When you scroll down past it and back, replay. Good.

Implementation detail: preserve initial `setShown(true)` fallback.

Let's edit.

